1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,643
Chapter one.

2
00:00:01,643 --> 00:00:03,559
The challenge.

3
00:00:04,010 --> 00:00:07,903
Some players thrive when the game is on the line.

4
00:00:07,903 --> 00:00:08,856
Others fade.

5
00:00:08,856 --> 00:00:15,132
Rajasthan Royals' SupeRR Selector Season 2 asks a precise question: using data,

6
00:00:15,132 --> 00:00:19,979
who are the most reliable batters and bowlers under pressure,

7
00:00:19,979 --> 00:00:25,699
and how could they strengthen Rajasthan Royals for the IPL 2027 auction?

8
00:00:25,699 --> 00:00:31,499
This film explains how we answered it, step by step, and how sure we are.

9
00:00:31,949 --> 00:00:34,048
Here is the answer up front.

10
00:00:34,048 --> 00:00:41,469
Our most reliable batter under pressure is Shreyas Iyer, with a reliability score of 97 out of 100.

11
00:00:41,469 --> 00:00:45,892
Our most reliable bowler is Varun Chakravarthy, scoring 96.

12
00:00:45,892 --> 00:00:56,612
And the evidence says Rajasthan Royals' two biggest needs are a finisher who holds up under pressure, and a bowler who can defend at the death.

13
00:00:56,612 --> 00:01:03,583
The rest of this documentary shows why, and where the evidence is strong and where it is not.

14
00:01:04,034 --> 00:01:05,919
Chapter two.

15
00:01:05,919 --> 00:01:07,333
The data.

16
00:01:07,783 --> 00:01:13,347
Our primary source is the official Rajasthan Royals data pack:

17
00:01:13,347 --> 00:01:19,001
ball-by-ball records for 200,961 deliveries across 867 matches,

18
00:01:19,001 --> 00:01:24,206
covering the IPL from 2022 to 2026 and T20 internationals.

19
00:01:24,206 --> 00:01:28,065
Before any modelling we audited every file.

20
00:01:28,065 --> 00:01:34,885
Computed scores matched the official result in 806 of 816 checkable matches,

21
00:01:34,885 --> 00:01:39,193
and the few exceptions were rain-affected games.

22
00:01:39,193 --> 00:01:44,936
We found a hidden no-ball code that, if treated as a legal ball,

23
00:01:44,936 --> 00:01:49,244
would have created hundreds of seven-ball overs.

24
00:01:49,244 --> 00:01:56,423
And we joined players only by ID, because some names belong to different people.

25
00:01:56,423 --> 00:02:03,872
Every model was validated chronologically: trained on 2022 to 2024, chosen on 2025,

26
00:02:03,872 --> 00:02:07,192
and tested on the unseen 2026 season.

27
00:02:07,643 --> 00:02:09,353
Chapter three.

28
00:02:09,353 --> 00:02:11,552
Defining pressure.

29
00:02:12,002 --> 00:02:16,961
Pressure cannot be observed directly, so we had to define it,

30
00:02:16,961 --> 00:02:21,025
using only information available before each ball.

31
00:02:21,025 --> 00:02:22,813
We built two measures.

32
00:02:22,813 --> 00:02:29,642
Stakes: a win-probability model tells us how much the next ball can swing the match.

33
00:02:29,642 --> 00:02:36,714
Difficulty: the scoreboard gap to an era-adjusted par, wickets lost, a recent collapse,

34
00:02:36,714 --> 00:02:41,429
a squeeze of dot balls, and how late it is in the innings.

35
00:02:41,429 --> 00:02:49,964
Stakes alone turned out to be blind to batting first: it put 92 percent of high-pressure balls in chases.

36
00:02:49,964 --> 00:02:57,768
So the final definition is half stakes and half difficulty, and the top 20 percent of all balls,

37
00:02:57,768 --> 00:03:00,531
40,193 of them, are high pressure.

38
00:03:00,982 --> 00:03:05,973
After a review, pressure now builds smoothly through both innings.

39
00:03:05,973 --> 00:03:13,007
And it changes behaviour: under high pressure batters were dismissed on 6.2 percent of balls,

40
00:03:13,007 --> 00:03:16,259
against 5.3 percent in ordinary situations.

41
00:03:16,259 --> 00:03:23,368
Raw strike rate is not the yardstick, because high-pressure balls cluster late in the innings,

42
00:03:23,368 --> 00:03:25,561
where everyone scores faster.

43
00:03:26,011 --> 00:03:27,342
Chapter four.

44
00:03:27,342 --> 00:03:30,516
Building the analytical engine.

45
00:03:30,966 --> 00:03:34,237
Fair comparison is the heart of the method.

46
00:03:34,237 --> 00:03:45,573
First, for every ball, a model estimates what an average player would produce in exactly that situation, using context only, never the player's name.

47
00:03:45,573 --> 00:03:53,638
Second, we score each ball as actual minus expected, charging a wicket at its real cost in that situation.

48
00:03:53,638 --> 00:03:58,583
Third, a ridge regression rates every batter and bowler together,

49
00:03:58,583 --> 00:04:03,224
so each player is judged net of the opponents actually faced.

50
00:04:03,224 --> 00:04:08,017
That is how IPL and international cricket are put on one scale.

51
00:04:08,017 --> 00:04:15,625
Fourth, small samples are pulled toward average, so nobody tops a list on a handful of good innings.

52
00:04:16,075 --> 00:04:17,724
Chapter five.

53
00:04:17,724 --> 00:04:20,135
Testing the models.

54
00:04:20,585 --> 00:04:24,722
A model is not included because it is advanced.

55
00:04:24,722 --> 00:04:45,759
For win probability, simple logistic regression beat LightGBM on both the 2025 validation season and the unseen 2026 test, with a chase Brier score of 0.116 against 0.139, because tree models could not extrapolate to 2026's record scoring.

56
00:04:45,759 --> 00:04:50,777
Validation also caught a bug: without an environment par,

57
00:04:50,777 --> 00:04:55,794
2026 batters would all have looked better than they were.

58
00:04:56,244 --> 00:05:00,033
Then the key scientific question: is clutch real?

59
00:05:00,033 --> 00:05:11,553
After removing sampling noise, only about 21 percent of a batter's raw pressure-versus-normal gap is genuine signal, and about 4 percent for bowlers.

60
00:05:11,553 --> 00:05:19,440
Performance in ordinary situations repeats from season to season; pressure-only form largely does not.

61
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:22,224
So we never rank a raw clutch table.

62
00:05:22,674 --> 00:05:28,066
Instead, the reliability score blends five things: pressure performance,

63
00:05:28,066 --> 00:05:35,406
consistency across pressure innings, impact on winning, the shrunk gap to a player's own baseline,

64
00:05:35,406 --> 00:05:37,129
and underlying ability.

65
00:05:37,129 --> 00:05:43,421
We stress-tested it with seven weightings and four alternative pressure definitions.

66
00:05:43,421 --> 00:05:49,038
The leaders hold; the middle of each list moves, and we report that openly.

67
00:05:49,488 --> 00:05:51,333
Chapter six.

68
00:05:51,333 --> 00:05:53,178
The batters.

69
00:05:53,628 --> 00:06:01,149
The ten most reliable batters under pressure: Shreyas Iyer, Heinrich Klaasen, Rajat Patidar,

70
00:06:01,149 --> 00:06:10,060
Namboori Tilak Varma, Ishan Kishan, Naman Dhir, David Miller, Tim David, Suryakumar Yadav and Glenn Phillips.

71
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:17,663
Heinrich Klaasen is the most certain pick, staying in the top ten in 92 percent of resamples.

72
00:06:17,663 --> 00:06:25,021
Andre Russell would have ranked fifth, but formally retired from the IPL in November 2025,

73
00:06:25,021 --> 00:06:28,782
so is excluded from a list built for IPL 2027.

74
00:06:29,232 --> 00:06:34,318
For each pick we name the two strongest players left out in the same role,

75
00:06:34,318 --> 00:06:37,067
and test whether the difference is real.

76
00:06:37,067 --> 00:06:42,565
Against those alternatives the top four batters are clearly or moderately ahead.

77
00:06:42,565 --> 00:06:45,658
Lower down the gaps shrink: for Ishan Kishan,

78
00:06:45,658 --> 00:06:50,812
the advantage over the best alternative is statistically indistinguishable,

79
00:06:50,812 --> 00:06:53,837
and we say so rather than pretend otherwise.

80
00:06:54,287 --> 00:06:56,262
Chapter seven.

81
00:06:56,262 --> 00:06:57,956
The bowlers.

82
00:06:58,406 --> 00:07:05,899
The ten most reliable bowlers under pressure: Varun Chakravarthy, Sunil Narine, Jasprit Bumrah,

83
00:07:05,899 --> 00:07:12,287
Lockie Ferguson, Rashid Khan, Axar Patel, Matheesha Pathirana, Bhuvneshwar Kumar,

84
00:07:12,287 --> 00:07:14,811
Kuldeep Yadav and Sikandar Raza.

85
00:07:14,811 --> 00:07:20,805
Varun Chakravarthy leads, staying in the top ten in 84 percent of resamples,

86
00:07:20,805 --> 00:07:27,431
with a moderate edge over the strongest alternative in the same role, Krunal Pandya.

87
00:07:27,881 --> 00:07:34,057
Every identity and availability flag was checked against official sources.

88
00:07:34,057 --> 00:07:39,900
Matheesha Pathirana was ruled out of IPL 2026 with a hamstring injury.

89
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:43,990
Kuldeep Yadav was traded to Lucknow Super Giants.

90
00:07:43,990 --> 00:07:51,919
Sikandar Raza has had no IPL contract since 2024, which makes Raza a potential auction entrant.

91
00:07:51,919 --> 00:07:57,595
These flags are reported beside the ranking, never used to score it.

92
00:07:58,045 --> 00:07:59,613
Chapter eight.

93
00:07:59,613 --> 00:08:01,965
The selection debate.

94
00:08:02,415 --> 00:08:03,676
How sure are we?

95
00:08:03,676 --> 00:08:05,646
Each dot is a final rank.

96
00:08:05,646 --> 00:08:12,425
The thick bar is the range from one hundred bootstrap resamples of the whole pipeline;

97
00:08:12,425 --> 00:08:17,627
the thin bar is the range across alternative pressure definitions.

98
00:08:17,627 --> 00:08:19,913
The top of each list is firm.

99
00:08:19,913 --> 00:08:24,879
From about sixth to fifteenth, players are statistically close.

100
00:08:25,329 --> 00:08:27,890
That is why the near misses matter.

101
00:08:27,890 --> 00:08:33,306
Abhishek Sharma and Mohsin Khan are each under one point from tenth place,

102
00:08:33,306 --> 00:08:38,429
and each beats the tenth-ranked player in about half of all resamples.

103
00:08:38,429 --> 00:08:41,649
They are credible alternatives, not rejects.

104
00:08:42,099 --> 00:08:43,426
Chapter nine.

105
00:08:43,426 --> 00:08:47,408
What do Rajasthan Royals actually need?

106
00:08:47,858 --> 00:08:55,943
We studied Rajasthan Royals' own squad before matching any candidate, using exactly the same framework.

107
00:08:55,943 --> 00:09:02,930
The batting is elite up front: first of ten for powerplay run rate in both 2025 and 2026.

108
00:09:02,930 --> 00:09:08,974
But at the death it ranked between seventh and tenth on run rate, boundaries,

109
00:09:08,974 --> 00:09:12,742
context value and wickets lost, in both seasons,

110
00:09:12,742 --> 00:09:18,472
and in 2025 the death bowling conceded runs faster than any other team's.

111
00:09:18,923 --> 00:09:26,093
Turning that into priorities, finishing batting scores 81 out of 100, death bowling 61,

112
00:09:26,093 --> 00:09:28,400
and middle-overs batting 60.

113
00:09:28,400 --> 00:09:33,427
Opening batting scores only 30: it is a strength, not a need.

114
00:09:33,427 --> 00:09:40,927
Among current players, only Ravindra Jadeja ranks in the bowling top twenty under pressure.

115
00:09:41,377 --> 00:09:42,860
Chapter ten.

116
00:09:42,860 --> 00:09:45,457
The auction strategy.

117
00:09:45,907 --> 00:09:49,188
Reliability and squad fit are kept separate.

118
00:09:49,188 --> 00:09:55,751
Squad fit asks which need a player would fill, how strong the evidence is in that phase,

119
00:09:55,751 --> 00:09:59,927
and whether an overseas player would cost a scarce slot.

120
00:09:59,927 --> 00:10:04,551
It guides targeting, but never reorders the competition lists.

121
00:10:05,001 --> 00:10:09,272
For batting, seven of the ten best fit the finishing role.

122
00:10:09,272 --> 00:10:21,276
Suryakumar Yadav, Tilak Varma and Shreyas Iyer are Indian finishing options that avoid the overseas squeeze; Tim David and Glenn Phillips are the overseas options.

123
00:10:21,726 --> 00:10:28,083
For bowling, Jasprit Bumrah fits the death-bowling need best, with Sunil Narine,

124
00:10:28,083 --> 00:10:33,089
Rashid Khan and Pathirana behind, subject to slots and fitness.

125
00:10:33,089 --> 00:10:38,413
Varun Chakravarthy would add middle-overs control alongside Jadeja.

126
00:10:38,413 --> 00:10:44,055
The run estimates in this table are hypothetical models, not forecasts.

127
00:10:44,505 --> 00:10:46,374
Chapter eleven.

128
00:10:46,374 --> 00:10:49,115
Limitations and risks.

129
00:10:49,565 --> 00:10:54,810
Pressure is a modelling choice, so we tested alternatives.

130
00:10:54,810 --> 00:11:01,955
The data covers 2022 to 2026, without domestic leagues or fielding attribution.

131
00:11:01,955 --> 00:11:09,462
Openers face few high-pressure balls, so some elite openers fall below eligibility.

132
00:11:09,462 --> 00:11:16,969
The IPL 2027 squads are not known until the retention deadline of 17 November 2026,

133
00:11:16,969 --> 00:11:23,029
and the 2027 purse is not verified, so we make no financial claims.

134
00:11:23,479 --> 00:11:25,248
Chapter twelve.

135
00:11:25,248 --> 00:11:27,844
Final recommendations.

136
00:11:28,293 --> 00:11:34,964
For Rajasthan Royals: target a finisher who holds up under pressure, and a death bowler,

137
00:11:34,964 --> 00:11:37,769
using the ranked shortlists for each.

138
00:11:37,769 --> 00:11:42,090
Add middle-overs spin control, and keep the opening pair.

139
00:11:42,090 --> 00:11:46,562
Re-run the needs analysis the day retentions are published.

140
00:11:46,562 --> 00:11:50,201
Reliable pressure performance is real but noisy.

141
00:11:50,201 --> 00:11:56,037
The defensible way to find it is to define pressure from the match situation,

142
00:11:56,037 --> 00:12:02,936
judge players against context and opponents, shrink small samples, and say how sure we are.

143
00:12:02,936 --> 00:12:05,058
That is SupeRR Intelligence.
